declare(strict_types=1);
echo '<div class="mk-prose">';
echo '<p class="mk-muted" style="margin-top:0;">The full history of Igbo writing — from Nsibidi to the Önwu orthography to Ńdébé — and the ongoing struggle to represent one of Africa\'s richest languages in its own script.</p>';

echo '<h2>The Central Problem</h2>';
echo '<p>Igbo is a language that has never been adequately written. Not because it lacks the resources for writing — it had Nsibidi, an ideographic system used across southeastern Nigeria for at least a thousand years, and the internal phonological sophistication of a language that evolved over millennia without need for external representation. The problem is that when written Igbo became necessary for missionary purposes in the 19th century, it was written in the Roman alphabet — a system designed for Latin and its European descendants, incapable of representing more than a fraction of Igbo\'s actual sound inventory.</p>';
echo '<p>The consequences persist. Every Igbo child who learns to read and write today learns an imprecise, distorting representation of their language — one that drops tones, collapses distinct consonants, and forces a phonologically rich oral tradition into a phonologically impoverished written form. The gap between spoken Igbo and written Igbo is not a pedagogical inconvenience; it is a structural diminishment of the language itself, one that contributes to the decline of Igbo literacy and the erosion of the language among younger generations.</p>';
echo '<p>This page traces the full history of that problem and the efforts — ancient and modern — to solve it.</p>';

echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
echo '<h2 style="color:#2d6a1f;">⊕ Nsibidi — The Ancient Script</h2>';

echo '<h3>Origin and History</h3>';
echo '<p>Nsibidi is the oldest known writing system of southeastern Nigeria — an ideographic and pictographic script that predates European contact by centuries, possibly by millennia. The earliest archaeological evidence for Nsibidi-like symbols comes from pottery and terracotta excavated in the Cross River region, with some scholars dating its origins to the mid-first millennium CE; if the inscriptions on the Ikom monoliths in Cross River State are accepted as Nsibidi, its origins may extend to 2000-3000 BCE.</p>';
echo '<p>Its origin is most consistently attributed to the Ejagham (Ekoi) people of the northern Cross River region, though a tradition recorded by J.K. Macgregor in the early 1900s attributed it to the Uguakima section of the Igbo. The Igbo connection is genuine regardless of origin: the Bende subgroup of Igbo speakers used Nsibidi, as did neighbouring Efik, Ibibio, and other Cross River communities. The system was shared across ethnic boundaries through the Ekpe (leopard) secret society, which used Nsibidi as both a practical communication system and an instrument of social and judicial control.</p>';

echo '<h3>What Nsibidi Is</h3>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>Ideographic, not phonetic</strong> — Nsibidi symbols represent ideas, actions, objects, and relationships rather than sounds. A symbol for "love" represents the concept of love regardless of how the word for love is pronounced in any given language. This made it usable across communities with mutually unintelligible languages — but it also means it cannot directly represent spoken language.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Over 1,000 symbols</strong> — the known corpus includes more than 1,000 symbols, of which over 500 have been documented and studied. Their subjects range from love and war to legal judgments, genealogy, and sacred knowledge.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Two levels</strong> — before the colonial era, Nsibidi had a sacred, esoteric level (restricted to Ekpe initiates) and a public, decorative level (used by women and non-initiates). The colonial period disrupted both levels, and much of the esoteric knowledge was lost.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Diaspora survival</strong> — Nsibidi was transported to Cuba and Haiti through the Atlantic slave trade, where it developed into the <em>anaforuana</em> symbols of the Abakuá secret society in Cuba and the <em>veve</em> symbols of Haitian Vodou. The Dora Milaje symbols in Marvel\'s <em>Black Panther</em> (2018) drew on Nsibidi imagery, bringing the script to global attention for the first time.</li>';
echo '</ul>';

echo '<h3>Why Nsibidi Cannot Solve the Writing Problem</h3>';
echo '<p>Nsibidi is a cultural treasure and an object of legitimate pride. It is not, however, a solution to the problem of writing Igbo speech. Because it is ideographic rather than phonetic, it cannot represent the tonal distinctions, the specific phoneme inventory, or the grammatical structures of the spoken Igbo language. It can communicate meaning without representing sound — which is precisely the limitation that a writing system for a tonal language must overcome. Nsibidi\'s survival is important for cultural continuity and esoteric tradition; it cannot replace a phonetic writing system.</p>';

echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
echo '<h2 style="color:#b7791f;">⊕ The Missionary Era — Roman Script Imposed</h2>';

echo '<h3>The First Written Igbo (1777-1850s)</h3>';
echo '<p>The first written representation of Igbo words appeared in G.C.A. Oldendorp\'s 1777 German missionary history — a handful of words transcribed using whatever combination of German and Latin letters seemed to approximate the sounds Oldendorp heard. This was not a writing system; it was a linguist\'s approximation, different from every other approximation anyone else made of the same sounds.</p>';
echo '<p>For the following century, every person who wrote Igbo used their own system. Missionaries from different denominations — Church Missionary Society (Anglican), Roman Catholic, Methodist — each developed their own orthographic conventions, producing a proliferation of incompatible spellings for the same words. A word written by one missionary was unrecognizable in another missionary\'s orthography. The result was not a written language but a collection of written approximations, each intelligible only within its own missionary tradition.</p>';

echo '<h3>Samuel Ajayi Crowther and the First Igbo Primer (1857)</h3>';
echo '<p>Samuel Ajayi Crowther — a Yoruba man, former slave, CMS missionary, and Africa\'s first Anglican bishop — published the first book in written Igbo in 1857: <em>Isoama-Igbo: A Primer</em>. He also published the first vocabulary of the Igbo language. Crowther was a linguistic genius who had already developed the first standardised Yoruba orthography and translated the Yoruba Bible — but his work on Igbo was constrained by the same Roman alphabet framework that constrained all missionary linguistic work of his era.</p>';
echo '<p>Crowther worked within the Lepsius Standard Alphabet — a system developed by the German philologist Karl Richard Lepsius in the 1850s as a universal scheme for representing African and Asian languages in Roman script. Lepsius\'s system was an improvement over ad hoc missionary orthographies, but it remained fundamentally a Roman alphabet with modifications — incapable of fully representing the phoneme inventory of a language like Igbo.</p>';

echo '<h3>The Union Igbo Controversy (1905-1929)</h3>';
echo '<p>In 1905, the CMS attempted to create a unified written Igbo by producing "Union Igbo" — an artificial standard dialect compiled from multiple Igbo dialects. The attempt failed. Union Igbo satisfied nobody: it did not correspond to the spoken Igbo of any community, it was rejected by communities who recognised neither its vocabulary nor its grammar as their own, and it demonstrated the fundamental impossibility of creating a "standard" dialect by administrative committee when the living dialects were too divergent.</p>';
echo '<p>In 1929, the International Institute of African Languages and Cultures produced the "Practical Orthography of African Languages" — an attempt to create a unified scheme for writing African languages across the continent. For Igbo, this was a partial improvement but remained within the Roman alphabet framework.</p>';

echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
echo '<h2 style="color:#553c9a;">⊕ The Önwu Orthography (1961) — Standardisation and Its Limits</h2>';

echo '<h3>The Committee and Its Work</h3>';
echo '<p>In 1961, one year after Nigerian independence, a committee chaired by F.C. Ogbalu produced the Önwu orthography — named after the Önwu (death) spirit, in the sense of something that overcomes and supersedes what came before. The committee\'s work produced the first genuinely standardised written Igbo, replacing the proliferation of missionary systems with a single agreed orthography. It is the system still in use today.</p>';
echo '<p>The Önwu orthography uses 24 of the 26 Roman letters (dropping Q and X) plus:</p>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>Four dotted vowels</strong>: ị, ọ, ụ, and ṅ — representing sounds that have no direct Roman equivalent</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Digraphs</strong>: two-letter combinations representing single sounds — gb, kp, nw, ny, gh, ch — that cannot be represented by single Roman letters</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Tone marks</strong>: acute accent (high tone), grave accent (low tone), and macron — which in practice are consistently omitted in most written Igbo because they are difficult to type and visually cluttered</li>';
echo '</ul>';

echo '<h3>What the Önwu System Gets Wrong</h3>';
echo '<p>The Önwu orthography was a significant achievement in its historical context. It also has structural limitations that cannot be remedied within the Roman alphabet framework:</p>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>The phoneme gap</strong> — Igbo has been estimated to have between 120 and 150 phonemes when all tonal distinctions are counted. The Önwu system represents approximately 36 phonemes — roughly a quarter of the full inventory. The remaining three-quarters of Igbo\'s sound distinctions are either lost in writing or forced into inadequate approximations.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Tone marking in practice</strong> — the system includes tone marks but they are almost universally omitted in practice because they are cumbersome to write by hand and difficult to type. The result is a written Igbo stripped of its tonal information — which for a tonal language is like writing English with all vowels removed. The word remains recognizable in context, but the systematic tonal information that disambiguates meaning is gone.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Dialect flattening</strong> — the Önwu orthography was based primarily on the Owerri dialect. Communities whose dialects differ significantly — Onitsha, Ngwa, Afikpo, Ika — find that standard written Igbo does not represent their speech. This is not a failure of the committee; it is an inherent limitation of any single orthography for a language with hundreds of dialects.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Digraph ambiguity</strong> — digraphs create parsing problems. Is "gb" one sound (labial-velar stop) or two consecutive sounds (g + b)? The context usually makes this clear to fluent readers but creates systematic difficulty for learners and for computational processing.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Trigraphs</strong> — some Igbo sounds require three Roman letters (trigraphs) for minimal representation: "nch," "ngw," and others. The system was not designed for trigraphs; their incorporation is ad hoc and inconsistent.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The missing phonemes</strong> — specific sounds in Igbo that the Önwu system cannot represent include: the labialized velar nasal (ŋʷ), the syllabic nasal in various positions, the downstep tone (distinct from both high and low), the upstep, aspirated stop variants, nasalized fricatives, and numerous dialect-specific phonemes. These are not marginal sounds — they are lexically and grammatically significant.</li>';
echo '</ul>';

echo '<h3>The Trigraph and Digraph Path Forward</h3>';
echo '<p>Within the Roman alphabet framework, the most direct path to greater phoneme coverage is systematic expansion of the digraph and trigraph system. The Önwu system uses 6 digraphs. A more complete Roman-based system could potentially represent 60-80 phonemes through systematic use of:</p>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>Additional digraphs</strong>: combinations like "bh," "dh," "gh" (beyond the current use), "kh," "lh," "mh," "nh," "rh" could represent aspirated and fricative variants</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Systematic trigraphs</strong>: "ngh," "ngw," "nkp," "mbw" and others for labialized and nasalized consonant clusters</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Diacritic expansion</strong>: beyond the four dotted vowels currently in use, systematic use of additional diacritics (breve, tilde, circumflex) could represent more vowel distinctions</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Tone standardisation</strong>: making tone marks mandatory in all formal written Igbo and creating keyboard standards that make them easy to type</li>';
echo '</ul>';
echo '<p>This path has the advantage of building on existing literacy: anyone who can read Önwu Igbo could learn an expanded system with additional characters. Its limitation is that it remains within a borrowed framework — a Roman alphabet that was never designed for Igbo and that carries phonological assumptions from Latin and European languages into the representation of a West African tonal language.</p>';

echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
echo '<h2 style="color:#2d6a1f;">⊕ Ńdébé — The Indigenous Solution (2009)</h2>';

echo '<h3>The Creator and the Creation</h3>';
echo '<p>In 2009, Lotanna Igwe-Odunze — a Nigerian software engineer and artist studying at Canisius College in Buffalo, New York — created the first draft of Ńdébé in one week during the American winter break. She was 19 years old. She published it on her blog on April 7, 2009, in a post titled "The Igbo Academy and the Ńdébé Project." The initial reaction from the Igbo community online was dismissive: "They laughed me out of the room," she recalled, "and it was all Igbo people." Over the following decade she refined, tested, and perfected the system, republishing it in 2020 as a fully developed standalone writing system.</p>';
echo '<p>The name Ńdébé comes from the Igbo word for "writing." The script itself combines ancient Igbo geometric design principles — related to the visual vocabulary of Nsibidi — with modern practicality: a system that can be learned efficiently, written by hand, typed on computers, and used to represent the full phoneme inventory of the Igbo language.</p>';

echo '<h3>What Ńdébé Does</h3>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>Full phoneme coverage</strong> — unlike the Önwu system, which represents approximately 36 phonemes, Ńdébé is designed to cover the full range of Igbo sounds, including the interchangeable sounds (R/L, F/H) that create dialect variation across Igboland</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Built-in tone notation</strong> — tone is encoded directly into the character shapes of Ńdébé, not added as a separate diacritic that writers routinely omit. The tone of every syllable is visually present in the character itself, making it impossible to write Ńdébé without tones</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Syllabary structure</strong> — Ńdébé is a syllabary (each character represents a syllable, not a single sound), similar in principle to Japanese hiragana/katakana. It has 1,174 characters in total, but only 97 must be memorized to read and write Igbo competently — the rest follow a systematic logic</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Consonant grid system</strong> — consonant shapes are designed using a systematic grid: stem + radical = consonant character. This logical structure means that knowing the system\'s logic allows you to derive unfamiliar characters rather than memorizing them individually</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Dialect flexibility</strong> — the system\'s ability to represent interchangeable sounds (R/L, F/H) means it can accommodate dialectal variation rather than forcing all dialects into a single standard</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Cultural continuity</strong> — the visual vocabulary of Ńdébé draws on Igbo geometric and design traditions, giving the script a distinctively Igbo aesthetic that connects it to the pre-colonial visual tradition</li>';
echo '</ul>';

echo '<h3>The Challenge of Adoption</h3>';
echo '<p>Ńdébé solves the technical problem of representing Igbo phonology. It has not yet solved the social problem of adoption. A writing system becomes useful only when enough people use it that texts written in it can be read by others — a threshold that requires institutional support, educational integration, and digital infrastructure (fonts, keyboards, Unicode encoding) that Ńdébé is still working toward.</p>';
echo '<p>The precedents are instructive: the Vai syllabary (Liberia, invented 1830, digitised 2008), the Bamum script (Cameroon, invented 1896, digitised 2009), the N\'Ko script (Guinea, invented 1949, now widely used across West Africa for Manding languages) — all demonstrate that indigenous African scripts can achieve genuine community use, but that the journey from invention to adoption takes generations and requires sustained institutional support.</p>';
echo '<p>Kola Tubosun, Nigerian linguist, described Ńdébé as offering "a writing system that addresses the tonal peculiarities of Nigerian languages, pleasing to the eye, which might carry the burden of our literary and academic aspirations." The question of whether Ńdébé will achieve the critical mass of users needed to fulfill that potential remains open.</p>';

echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
