  ['1777','G.C.A. Oldendorp (German missionary)','First written representation of Igbo words — in a German missionary history; not a system, a transcription'],
  ['1850s','Karl Richard Lepsius (German philologist)','Developed the Lepsius Standard Alphabet — used as framework for early missionary Igbo orthography'],
  ['1857','Samuel Ajayi Crowther (Yoruba linguist/bishop)','Published <em>Isoama-Igbo</em> — first book in written Igbo; first Igbo vocabulary; established Roman script precedent'],
  ['1905','CMS committee','"Union Igbo" — failed attempt at standardisation by artificial dialect blending; rejected by all communities'],
  ['1929','International Institute of African Languages','"Practical Orthography of African Languages" — partial improvement; still within Roman framework'],
  ['1961','F.C. Ogbalu (chair) and Önwu Committee','Önwu orthography — current standard; 24 Roman letters + dotted vowels + digraphs; represents ~36 of ~150 phonemes'],
  ['1972','Kay Williamson (British linguist)','Igbo-English dictionary; rigorous phonological analysis; most complete scholarly documentation of Igbo tones'],
  ['2009','Lotanna Igwe-Odunze (Nigerian engineer/artist)','Invented Ńdébé — first indigenous syllabic script for Igbo; full phoneme coverage; built-in tone notation'],
  ['2020','Lotanna Igwe-Odunze','Ńdébé relaunched as standalone system independent of Nsibidi; book published; global attention'],
  ['Ongoing','Nsibidi Unicode campaign','Advocacy to include Nsibidi in Unicode standard for digital use across devices and platforms'],
];
