echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
echo '<h2>The Unrepresented Phonemes — What Writing Still Cannot Say</h2>';

echo '<p>The specific phonemes that the Önwu orthography cannot represent include:</p>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>The downstep tone</strong> (↓) — a mid-pitch tone that falls between high and low and is distinct from both; written Igbo collapses this into either high or low, losing a lexically significant distinction</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The upstep</strong> — the reverse of downstep; similarly lost in current orthography</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Syllabic nasals</strong> — the nasal consonant functioning as a syllable nucleus (as in "m" before "b" in many Igbo words) is inconsistently represented</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Labialized velars</strong> — the sounds kʷ, gʷ, ŋʷ (velar consonants with simultaneous lip rounding) present in many dialects and not representable in the standard system</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Aspirated stops</strong> — Igbo has aspirated variants of /p/, /t/, /k/ in some dialects and words; these are collapsed with their unaspirated counterparts in writing</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Nasalized vowels</strong> — vowels pronounced with nasal resonance; distinguished in speech, merged in writing</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Vowel length</strong> — some Igbo dialects distinguish long and short vowels; the current system has no mechanism for representing length</li>';
echo '<li><strong>The full consonant inventory</strong> — estimates of Igbo\'s consonant phonemes (including all dialect variants) range from 36 to over 60; the Önwu system represents 28</li>';
echo '</ul>';
echo '<p>Each unrepresented phoneme is a meaning distinction lost in writing. When a child learns written Igbo and encounters a word, they cannot reliably reconstruct its pronunciation from its spelling — a problem that does not exist in adequately phonetic writing systems and that systematically undermines the connection between written and spoken Igbo.</p>';

echo '<h2>The Path Forward</h2>';
echo '<p>Three paths are available, and they are not mutually exclusive:</p>';
echo '<ol>';
echo '<li><strong>Expand the Önwu system</strong> — systematic development of additional digraphs, trigraphs, and diacritics within the Roman framework. Least disruptive to existing literacy; most limited in ultimate phoneme coverage. Requires institutional consensus from SPILC and related bodies.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Adopt Ńdébé</strong> — transition to the indigenous syllabic system with full phoneme coverage. Requires new literacy acquisition for all existing Igbo readers; requires digital infrastructure (Unicode, fonts, keyboards); requires educational integration. The long-term solution if it achieves institutional support.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Develop Nsibidi for modern use</strong> — invest in Nsibidi documentation, standardisation, and digital encoding (Unicode), making it available as a cultural and creative system while using Ńdébé or an expanded Önwu for phonetic writing. Preserves the ancestral script while acknowledging its ideographic limitations for phonetic representation.</li>';
echo '</ol>';
echo '<p>What is certain is that the status quo — a 24-letter Roman alphabet that captures one-quarter of Igbo\'s phonemes, with tone marks routinely omitted — is not adequate to the task of preserving and transmitting a language of 45 million speakers. The question is not whether the Igbo writing system needs to change but what form that change should take and who has the authority to implement it.</p>';

